Multiple FUNCTIONS (p.740)
In the strip -\frac{\pi}{2}<x_{0}<\frac{\pi}{2}, the imaginary zeros of ce_t(z, q), se_r(z, q) are the real zeros of Ce_t(z, q), Se_t(z, q), hence also the real zeros of Mc_t^{(1)}(z, q) and Ms_t^{(1)}(z, q), respectively.

For small q, the large zeros of Ce_t(z, q), Se_t(z, q) approach the zeros of J_t(2\sqrt{q}\cosh z).

Table 1: The [20.56] tabulates the first "non-trivial" zero (i.e. different from 0, \frac{\pi}{2},\pi\)) for ce_t(z), se_t(z), r=2(1)5 and for se_t(z) to within \({}^{6}10^{-4}\); for q=0(1)10(2)40. He also gives the "turning" points (zeros of the derivative) and also expansions for them for small q. Wiltse and King [20.61,2] tabulate the first two (non-trivial) zeros of Mc_t^{(1)}(z, q) and M_s^{(2)}(z, q) and of their derivatives r=0, 1, 2 for 6 or 7 values of q between .25 and 10. The graphs reproduced here indicate their location.

Between two real zeros of Mc_t^{(1)}(z, q), M_s^{(1)}(z, q) there is a zero of Mc_t^{(2)}(z, q), M_s^{(2)}(z, q), respectively. No tabulation of such zeros exists yet.

Available tables are described in the References.

The most comprehensive tabulation of the characteristic values a, b, (in a somewhat different notation) and of the coefficients proportional to A_m_ and B_m as defined in 20.5.4 and 20.5.5 can be found in [20.58]. In addition, the table contains certain important "joining factors", with the aid of which it is possible to obtain values of Mc_t^{(2)}(z, q) and M_s^{(1)}(z, q). as well as their derivatives, at x=0. Values of the functions ce_t(x, q)_ and se_t(x, q) for orders up to five or six can be found in [20.56]. Tabulations of less extensive character, but important in some aspects, are outlined in the other references cited. In this chapter only representative values of the various functions are given, along with several graphs.
